{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1090,1228p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1090\t  and 2 run once, at `validated`; acts 3 and 4 run once per\n  1091\t  receiving task -- the parent at `linked`, and each waiter or later\n  1092\t  requirer at its own restore.\n  1093\t  1. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\n  1094\t     (section 7.5.1, amended below): each closure becomes a\n  1095\t     `:yin.k/closure` marker naming its **origin image** and its entry\n  1096\t     relative to that image, with its captured values encoded\n  1097\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\n  1098\t     origin is not assumed to be the module's own image: a closure the\n  1099\t     child obtained from a dependency and re-exported originates in\n  1100\t     that dependency's image, and the lift records whichever image the\n  1101\t     closure's coordinates fall in. The lift is refused as\n  1102\t     `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF refuses (a host\n  1103\t     object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift refuses the\n  1104\t     install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not an export.\n  1105\t  2. *The origin set is checked.* The lifted slice's\n  1106\t     `:yin.k/requires :yin.k/segments` enumerates every origin image it\n  1107\t     names, transitively through captured values. Each must be an image\n  1108\t     this scheduler verified: the module's own, or one delivered for an\n  1109\t     install in this scheduler's history (held under `:installs` or the\n  1110\t     registry by identity). An origin identity the scheduler never\n  1111\t     verified is `:foreign-image`, and the install is refused; a\n  1112\t     verified image is never re-fetched for this step.\n  1113\t  3. *The receiving task attaches every origin image its kernel does\n  1114\t     not yet hold*, non-destructively. The ordinary loaders\n  1115\t     (`vm-load-rows`, `load-vector`, `load-image`) are entry points\n  1116\t     for a fresh run: they set the program counter, control, or root,\n  1117\t     and reset the operand stack and frames. A receiving task at its\n  1118\t     restore is a parked task whose registers must survive, so each\n  1119\t     kernel exposes a second\n  1120\t     operation, **`attach-image`**, that extends the kernel's code\n  1121\t     space and touches nothing else. Its contract, per kernel:\n  1122\t     - *semantic*: the verified vector is added under a fresh local id\n  1123\t       and the address is written to the alias column; `:control`,\n  1124\t       `:env`, `:stack`, `:k`, every `:return` frame's segment and pc,\n  1125\t       and the store are unchanged, because existing local ids are\n  1126\t       never renumbered (UCF section 7.3.4, \"local-id immutability\n  1127\t       stands\");\n  1128\t     - *stack* and *register*: the image is relocated by the current\n  1129\t       held length and appended to `:segment` (and, for the register\n  1130\t       VM, `:bodies` shifted likewise), and one `[identity offset\n  1131\t       length]` row is appended to the **offset table**, a kernel-state\n  1132\t       vector the lift of act 1 also reads, whose first row is the\n  1133\t       base image `[H 0 n]` recorded by `load-image`, so the table is\n  1134\t       the kernel's complete image inventory; `:pc`, `:stack`,\n  1135\t       `:frames`, `:continuation` (every `:return-pc` and `:frames`\n  1136\t       in it), and `:registers` are unchanged, because appending never\n  1137\t       moves an instruction already held; `:hash` becomes the identity\n  1138\t       of the new concatenation, as `yin.repl`'s `append-*-image`\n  1139\t       computes it, and is from r6 on **never a restore key**: a\n  1140\t       concatenation identity changes at every attach, so an entry or\n  1141\t       continuation that recorded it would be refused by the first\n  1142\t       later attach;\n  1143\t     - *walker*: the verified tree's rows are added to the kernel's\n  1144\t       row set (`:code-segment` expansion) keyed by their ids; the\n  1145\t       current node, environment, and continuation are unchanged.\n  1146\t     `yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` and `append-register-image`\n  1147\t     already do the relocation and append but then set `:pc` to the\n  1148\t     new offset to start the next input; `attach-image` is that\n  1149\t     function without the final `:pc` assignment, and `yin.repl` is\n  1150\t     rewritten to call `attach-image` and then set `:pc` itself.\n  1151\t\n  1152\t     **Hash-safe restore (r6).** A parked or waiting entry records,\n  1153\t     beside `:format`, the **image identity** its positions are\n  1154\t     relative to -- the offset-table row its `:pc` falls in -- and\n  1155\t     restores against the table, never against `:hash`.\n  1156\t     `stack-restore` and `register-restore` today refuse any entry\n  1157\t     whose `:hash` differs from the loaded image's and then write\n  1158\t     `:segment` back from the entry, so an attach mid-park would make\n  1159\t     every parked entry unrestorable or rewind the kernel to the\n  1160\t     pre-attach concatenation, discarding images other entries and\n  1161\t     closures still name (the semantic entry needs no change: its\n  1162\t     `:segment` is a local id that attach never renumbers). From r6\n  1163\t     the identity check is table membership -- the row exists, the\n  1164\t     table only grows, and an absent row is a host assembly defect --\n  1165\t     and the entry restores registers and never assigns `:segment`:\n  1166\t     the code space is kernel state, and since appending moves\n  1167\t     nothing, the entry's absolute pcs and stack bases still name\n  1168\t     what they named. A lifted continuation (act 1) carries its pcs\n  1169\t     image-relatively, as `[identity rel-pc]` pairs for `:pc`, every\n  1170\t     `:return-pc`, and every body reference, so a lower into another\n  1171\t     kernel rebases each by that kernel's table row; this is the\n  1172\t     rebasing r5 lacked, and same-kernel restore needs none of it.\n  1173\t     An image already present is not attached twice; the alias column\n  1174\t     or the offset table says so.\n  1175\t\n  1176\t     **Grow-only returns (r7).** Parked restores are one restore path;\n  1177\t     ordinary function returns are the other, and r6 left them alone.\n  1178\t     The register VM's call frame today carries the caller's whole\n  1179\t     `:segment` and `:hash` (its `:call` builds the frame), and its\n  1180\t     return transition writes both back into the VM\n  1181\t     (`return-transition`): an image a nested require attached while\n  1182\t     the call was in flight is discarded the moment the callee\n  1183\t     returns, though the grown code space is exactly what the\n  1184\t     resumed caller must run in. From r7 one rule covers every\n  1185\t     transition: **no frame, entry, or restore assigns a code-space\n  1186\t     value; `:segment`, `:hash`, and the offset table are kernel\n  1187\t     state that only the loaders and `attach-image` write.** The\n  1188\t     register frame drops `:segment` and `:hash` -- its absolute\n  1189\t     `:return-pc` survives every later append -- and its return\n  1190\t     restores pc, frames, registers, and continuation only. The\n  1191\t     stack frame already conforms (`:return-pc`, `:frames`,\n  1192\t     `:stack-base`; its `:return` never touches `:segment`), and the\n  1193\t     semantic frame's `:segment` is a local id that attach never\n  1194\t     renumbers. A lift that needs a frame pc's image derives the row\n  1195\t     from the offset table at lift time, so frames carry no identity\n  1196\t     either.\n  1197\t  4. *Each receiving task lowers the encoding* against its own\n  1198\t     coordinate space by the mapping below and writes the result into\n  1199\t     its own `:modules` entry as `:bindings`, which is what\n  1200\t     `resolve-var`'s module step reads. The scheduler's registry\n  1201\t     value never holds lowered bindings: it gains `{'foo {:manifest m\n  1202\t     :address a :derivation d :slice lifted :stores {addr store}}}`,\n  1203\t     the portable encoding of acts 1 to 3. Revision r5 lowered once\n  1204\t     into one parent's coordinates and published the result to every\n  1205\t     waiter; two tasks holding images of different lengths attach\n  1206\t     the same origin at different offsets and mint different local\n  1207\t     ids, so one task's lowered slice would misplace every body pc\n  1208\t     and alias in the other's. Every waiter is restored with `'foo`\n  1209\t     as its value after its own attach and lower; so is every later\n  1210\t     requirer.\n  1211\t\n  1212\t  **The closure marker, and its lift and lower per binding discipline.**\n  1213\t  UCF section 7.5.1 defines the closure marker for the named semantic\n  1214\t  VM. The de Bruijn kernels are positional and nameless, so the marker\n  1215\t  gains a `:yin.k/binding` key with two variants, and a module's\n  1216\t  closures carry the `:yin.k/store-of` key of the module store; both\n  1217\t  are amendments to UCF listed in section 11, item 12:\n  1218\t\n  1219\t  ```clojure\n  1220\t  ;; named (:yin.ast/code, :yin.semantic/code)\n  1221\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :named\n  1222\t   :yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc\n  1223\t   :yin.k/params [sym ...] :yin.k/env {sym encoded}\n  1224\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1225\t\n  1226\t  ;; positional (:yin.debruijn.code, :yin.debruijn.register)\n  1227\t  {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure :yin.k/binding :positional\n  1228\t   :yin.k/segment identity :yin.k/entry pc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
