{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1228,1395p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1228\t   :yin.k/segment identity :yin.k/entry pc\n  1229\t   :yin.k/arity n :yin.k/frames [[encoded ...] ...]\n  1230\t   :yin.k/store-of m}                       ; when module-defined\n  1231\t  ```\n  1232\t\n  1233\t  | Kernel closure                       | Lift                          |\n  1234\t  |--------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n  1235\t  | semantic `{:type :closure :params p  | `addr` = alias column inverse |\n  1236\t  | :entry e :segment local :env E}`     | of `local`; `pc` = `e`;       |\n  1237\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1238\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1239\t  | walker `{:type :closure :params p    | `addr` = the closure's        |\n  1240\t  | :body node :lambda id :env E}` (the  | recorded `:lambda` row id,    |\n  1241\t  | body is an AST node, not a code      | verified against `node` and   |\n  1242\t  | coordinate)                          | `p` by the rule below;        |\n  1243\t  |                                      | `pc` = `nil`;                 |\n  1244\t  |                                      | `params` = `p`; `env` =       |\n  1245\t  |                                      | `encode(E)`                   |\n  1246\t  | stack / register `{:type :closure    | offset-table entry with       |\n  1247\t  | :arity n :body-pc p :frames F}`      | `off <= p < off + len` gives  |\n  1248\t  |                                      | `identity` and `pc = p - off`;|\n  1249\t  |                                      | `arity` = `n`; `frames` =     |\n  1250\t  |                                      | `mapv (mapv encode) F`,       |\n  1251\t  |                                      | outermost frame first         |\n  1252\t\n  1253\t  | Marker variant | Lower into the parent                              |\n  1254\t  |----------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n  1255\t  | `:named`,      | `local'` = parent alias column at `addr`; `:entry` |\n  1256\t  | semantic       | unchanged; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =           |\n  1257\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1258\t  | `:named`,      | `:body` = the body slot of the parent's row at     |\n  1259\t  | walker         | `addr`, reconstructed as a node by the walker's    |\n  1260\t  |                | row decoder; `:params` unchanged; `:env` =         |\n  1261\t  |                | `decode(env)`                                      |\n  1262\t  | `:positional`  | `off'` = parent offset-table entry for `identity`; |\n  1263\t  |                | `:body-pc` = `off' + pc`; `:arity` = `n`;          |\n  1264\t  |                | `:frames` = `mapv (mapv decode) frames`            |\n  1265\t\n  1266\t  **The walker rule, exactly.** A walker closure holds its body as a\n  1267\t  live AST node, so its code coordinate is not a field of the closure\n  1268\t  but a fact about the rows: the body is the `body` slot of a\n  1269\t  `:lambda` row in the rows the child holds, and that row has an id\n  1270\t  (its `segment-key`, `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 4.1). The\n  1271\t  row is **not** found by the node alone: rows are content-addressed\n  1272\t  and shared, and `semantic-bytecode->ast` rebuilds \"a row reached\n  1273\t  through several parents ... once and shared\", so one body node can\n  1274\t  sit under several `:lambda` rows with different params, and a\n  1275\t  `node -> row id` index cannot select among them. The walker's\n  1276\t  decoder therefore records the **source `:lambda` row id** in the\n  1277\t  closure when it builds it -- the decoder is decoding that very row,\n  1278\t  so the id is in hand -- and the lift reads the recorded id and\n  1279\t  verifies the row's body slot is the closure's node and its params\n  1280\t  are the closure's params; a disagreement is the same\n  1281\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` `:unrooted-body` refusal as an id in no held\n  1282\t  row. A closure with no recorded id, which only a host function can\n  1283\t  fabricate, resolves through the walker's row index keyed by the\n  1284\t  pair `[node params]` (the index `vm-load-rows` builds when it\n  1285\t  decodes rows into nodes and `attach-image` extends), and refuses\n  1286\t  when the pair matches no row. The origin image of a walker closure\n  1287\t  is the tree whose rows contain that `:lambda` row; the `:lambda`\n  1288\t  row id is content-addressed, so the same lambda shared by two\n  1289\t  trees is one id and either tree serves as origin. The lower reads\n  1290\t  the parent's row at that id (present after act 3) and decodes its\n  1291\t  body slot into a node through the same decoder the loader uses, so\n  1292\t  the closure's `:body` in the parent is structurally equal to the\n  1293\t  child's and its `:params` and decoded `:env` are carried unchanged.\n  1294\t\n  1295\t  A `:named` marker lowers only into a named kernel and a `:positional`\n  1296\t  marker only into a positional kernel of the same `:format`; the\n  1297\t  reverse is `:binding-mismatch`. This never arises within one install,\n  1298\t  because the child is a VM of the same backend as the parent (section\n  1299\t  7.3, first rule); it is stated so that a lifted slice carried\n  1300\t  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\n  1301\t  closure between disciplines is a re-lowering of source under a\n  1302\t  derivation record (section 8.1), never a relocation. `decode` and\n  1303\t  `encode` recurse through frames and environments, so a captured\n  1304\t  closure is relocated by the same rules and its origin joins the\n  1305\t  origin set of act 2.\n  1306\t\n  1307\t  **The module store (r6, amended r7).** A child runs with an\n  1308\t  isolated store, and its exports must not leak that isolation. A\n  1309\t  module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports `(fn [] x)` publishes a\n  1310\t  closure whose free read of `x` would, if only the closure value\n  1311\t  crossed the boundary, resolve through the receiving task's store\n  1312\t  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\n  1313\t  `x` is unbound or bound to something the publisher never saw. The\n  1314\t  stores therefore cross with the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's\n  1315\t  slice discipline:\n  1316\t  - *Lift.* One snapshot is encoded per module, not just the child's\n  1317\t    own: the child's halted store under its manifest address, and\n  1318\t    for every dependency whose store the exports reach transitively\n  1319\t    (a re-exported closure carries that dependency's `:store-of`),\n  1320\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\n  1321\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\n  1322\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\n  1323\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\n  1324\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  1325\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\n  1326\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\n  1327\t    (a host handle the body parked on) refuses the lift and the\n  1328\t    install.\n  1329\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\n  1330\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\n  1331\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\n  1332\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\n  1333\t    closure.\n  1334\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\n  1335\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\n  1336\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\n  1337\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\n  1338\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\n  1339\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\n  1340\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\n  1341\t    7.6.2's \"there is no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a\n  1342\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n  1343\t    the module's value.\n  1344\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\n  1345\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\n  1346\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\n  1347\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\n  1348\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\n  1349\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\n  1350\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it, falling through to the\n  1351\t    ambient store only for a key it lacks, a resource cell the\n  1352\t    engine itself filed under a gensym'd id that no program names\n  1353\t    as a store key; the engine's own bookkeeping is untouched. The\n  1354\t    context threads through calls: a frame records the caller's\n  1355\t    active `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that\n  1356\t    closure's `:store-of` active, and returning restores the\n  1357\t    frame's, so a module closure calling another module's closure\n  1358\t    routes each body's reads and writes to its own module. Sibling\n  1359\t    exports in one task share one store instance, so a write one\n  1360\t    makes is visible to the next; each task holds its own\n  1361\t    instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared obligation\n  1362\t    the store does not hold falls through to the receiver's\n  1363\t    bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a conditional\n  1364\t    definition that ran overwrites the module's own slot, which is\n  1365\t    the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\n  1366\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\n  1367\t    dependency's closure runs against the dependency's store\n  1368\t    instance in the child's state, its mutations are what the lift\n  1369\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\n  1370\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\n  1371\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1372\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1373\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1374\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1375\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1376\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1377\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1378\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1379\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1380\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1381\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1382\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1383\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1384\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1385\t  again.\n  1386\t\n  1387\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1388\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1389\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1390\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1391\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1392\tgrain.\n  1393\t\n  1394\t### 7.4 Transitive requires and cycles\n  1395\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
